**1. Analysis of the attached PDFs:**

*   **Summary of "Journal of Controlled Release 204 (2015) 51-59":**
    This paper introduces Nano-11, a positively charged, dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, as a vaccine adjuvant candidate. It demonstrates that Nano-11 efficiently adsorbs negatively charged protein antigens like ovalbumin and is readily phagocytized by dendritic cells (DCs). Nano-11 activates DCs, evidenced by increased expression of costimulatory molecules (CD80, CD86) and secretion of IL-1β and IL-12p40. *In vivo*, Nano-11 significantly enhances antibody responses to protein antigens, while inducing only a transient, mild inflammatory response at the injection site that resolves quickly.

*   **Summary of "npj Vaccines (2017)2:4":**
    This study further characterizes Nano-11's *in vivo* properties, showing its adjuvanticity is comparable to aluminum hydroxide, eliciting robust IgG1 responses. Nano-11 remains largely localized at the injection site, gradually clearing over three weeks without systemic distribution. It induces a transient local inflammatory response dominated by monocytes/macrophages, with few neutrophils. Crucially, Nano-11 is efficiently taken up and transported by migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) to draining lymph nodes, delivering antigen more effectively per cell and enhancing MigDC maturation, confirming its role as a promising and safe vaccine adjuvant.

